<!-- Nomad vs Saily Comparison Content -->
<div class="content-section">

  {% set nomadBest = providerA.codes | findCode("TRAVELESIMEXPERT") %}
  {% set sailyPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Saily</strong> if you're buying data for a normal trip and want the lower price, plus ad blocking and phishing protection you'd otherwise pay a VPN for. Pick <strong>Nomad</strong> if you'll be abroad for months and would rather pay once for a big bundle that stays valid for up to a year than keep buying small plans or sign up for a monthly subscription. On a single small plan, Saily is cheaper in every destination below, even after Nomad's 20% Global-plan code. On US and Europe plans, Nomad's flat $3 USD REG3 code could close the gap.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: One Big Prepaid Bundle or a Monthly Subscription</h2>
  <p>
    Both sell ordinary country, regional, and global plans through an app, so the interesting split is what each one offers someone who travels for a long time. Nomad's answer is <strong>Global-EX</strong>: a prepaid bundle you buy once. On Nomad's own plan page today that's 10GB for 180 days at $39, or 20GB, 30GB, or 50GB valid for 365 days at $59, $79, and $129, covering 98 countries. The 50GB bundle works out to $2.58/GB, against $5.15/GB at the cheap end of Nomad's standard Global plan. Nothing renews and nothing bills you again.
  </p>
  <p>
    Saily's answer is a subscription instead: <strong>Saily Ultra</strong> at $59.99/month bundles 30GB of high-speed data across 121+ destinations (then unlimited at 1Mbps), the full NordVPN/NordPass/NordLayer/Incogni suite, 8% cashback in Saily credit, and one airport lounge pass plus one fast-track security pass per month. That's good value only if you'd use the extras. If you just want a lot of data for a long trip, Nomad's one-off bundle is the simpler buy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Saily is built in-house by Nord Security (the company behind NordVPN), so every plan, not just Ultra, ships with ad blocking, malware/phishing site blocking, and a virtual-location tool. It also uses one universal eSIM that keeps working as you cross into another supported country, rather than a separate profile per region.
  </p>

