      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="content-note">With Saily's 15% code, its 1GB plans come to about $3.39 in the US, $4.24 in Europe and $7.64 on Global. Nomad's 20% code (TRAVELESIMEXPERT) only applies to Global plans, which puts a 1GB Global plan at $9.60, so Saily stays cheaper there. On US and Europe plans, Nomad's only working code is REG3, a flat $3 USD off that we tested on a larger plan, so confirm what it does to a 1GB plan before assuming either side wins. Nomad's prices are from its own plan pages this month; Saily's are from our <a href="/saily/">Saily page</a>, since saily.com blocks our automated checks. Confirm the live price at checkout either way.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Discount Codes</h2>
  <div class="content-box content-box--tan">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nomad:</strong> TRAVELESIMEXPERT, {{ nomadBest.discount | lower }}. <a href="/nomad/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><strong>Saily:</strong> {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, {{ sailyPrimary.discount | lower }}. <a href="/saily/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Neither code is ours. Neither Nomad nor Saily runs the kind of affiliate program that gives us a code of our own, so these are the best public codes on each provider's page. Nomad's best code only applies to Global plans, and its flat $3 USD code (REG3) works on any plan.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Nomad or Saily the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Nomad if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You're abroad for months and want one prepaid bundle valid for up to 365 days, with no subscription</li>
        <li>You want to test the service first: new customers can claim a free 1GB trial eSIM</li>
        <li>You want 24/7 support over live chat as well as email</li>
        <li>You only need a little Europe data on an ongoing basis: the Nomad Pass, a $3 subscription that renews every 30 days, gives 1GB of Europe regional data each cycle</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Saily if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want the lower price on a normal-sized trip, even after Nomad's bigger code</li>
        <li>You'd normally pay for a VPN or ad blocker separately when traveling</li>
        <li>You're crossing several borders and don't want a new eSIM in each region</li>
        <li>You fly often enough that Ultra's monthly lounge and fast-track passes would actually get used</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Both sell unlimited plans now, but only Saily publishes the throttle point.</strong> Nomad's plan pages list short unlimited passes (in the US: 3 days for $11, 5 days for $16, 7 days for $23; in Europe: 5 days for $19, 10 days for $34) without stating a fair-use limit on the page. Saily's fair-use policy is specific: full speed for the first 5GB in a rolling 24-hour window in most destinations (3GB in some), then 1Mbps until the local-midnight reset. If you're planning to hotspot a laptop all day, that published number is worth knowing in advance.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Saily can't be bought or topped up while you're physically in Turkey.</strong> Its website and app are among those Turkey's telecom regulator (BTK) has blocked since mid-2025. A Saily eSIM bought and installed before you land keeps working, so this only bites if you planned to buy on arrival.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both providers support hotspot/tethering. If long-term data is the deciding factor, our <a href="/compare/nomad-vs-ubigi/">Nomad vs Ubigi comparison</a> sets Global-EX against another subscription-style option. For device setup, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Nomad vs Saily FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Nomad or Saily</h3>
      <p>Saily, on single small plans. A 1GB plan costs $3.99 in the US, $4.99 for Europe, and $8.99 global on Saily, versus $5, $5.50, and $12 on Nomad. On a 1GB Global plan Saily stays cheaper even after Nomad's 20% code (Global plans only) and Saily's 15% code; on US and Europe plans, Nomad's flat $3 USD REG3 code could close the gap, and we have only tested it on a larger plan. Nomad wins on bulk long-stay data: its 50GB Global-EX bundle works out to $2.58/GB.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is better for months abroad</h3>
      <p>Nomad, if you just want data. Its Global-EX bundles (10GB to 50GB, up to 365 days, 98 countries) are a single prepaid purchase with nothing to cancel. Saily's long-term option is the $59.99/month Ultra subscription, which only makes sense if you'd use its bundled NordVPN suite and airport perks.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Do Nomad and Saily have unlimited plans</h3>
      <p>Both do. Nomad sells short unlimited passes, for example 3, 5, or 7 days in the US and 5 or 10 days in Europe, and doesn't list a fair-use limit on those plan pages. Saily's unlimited plans run at full speed for 5GB per rolling 24 hours in most destinations (3GB in some), then 1Mbps until local midnight.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I use hotspot on Nomad and Saily</h3>
      <p>Yes. Both providers support hotspot/tethering, and Nomad also lets a plan's data be shared across multiple devices.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

